  tags={['Moderation', 'Platform Safety', 'Scale']}
>
  <p>
    When online communities scale from thousands to millions, traditional moderation breaks down predictably. 
    Platforms tend to react in two unhelpful ways: either relying entirely on blunt automated keyword filters, 
    or burning out human moderators with impossible queues of flagged content.
  </p>

  <h2>The Limits of Binary Moderation</h2>
  <p>
    Most moderation setups rely on a blunt binary: permitted versus banned. This misses how people actually communicate. 
    The majority of problematic interactions come from miscommunication, cultural misunderstandings, 
    or escalating arguments, rather than dedicated bad actors.
  </p>

  <h2>The 3-Tier Moderation Setup</h2>
  <p>
    To protect platform safety without alienating users, we use a three-tier operational structure:
  </p>

  <h3>Tier 1: Algorithmic Triage and Pattern Filtering</h3>
  <p>
    Automated tools should not make final calls on nuanced discussions. Their job is triage: 
    filtering obvious spam runs, harmful links, and explicit violations, while passing ambiguous edge cases 
    to human reviewers with full context attached.
  </p>

  <h3>Tier 2: Community-Led Moderation</h3>
  <p>
    Empowering trusted community members and volunteer leads with lightweight tools: slow modes, cooling-off 
    periods, and basic dispute handling. When community guidelines are upheld by active peers rather than distant corporate administrators, 
    users are far more receptive.
  </p>

  <h3>Tier 3: Specialist Safety Operations</h3>
  <p>
    A dedicated professional team trained in conflict resolution, legal compliance, and emergency response. 
    This team handles severe violations, coordinated harassment, and complex policy decisions.
  </p>

  <h2>Protecting Moderator Health</h2>
  <p>
    Sustainable safety operations require genuine support for the people reviewing content. Shift limits, 
    regular debriefs, and rotation between queue work and non-incident tasks are basic operational necessities.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
